@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-requirements Requirements
@brief Requirements are checks that stop the test case when they fail.

@details
When a precondition of the following checks does not hold, the test case
cannot continue meaningfully. A failed requirement is reported and counted
like a failed expectation, and then the rest of the test case body is
skipped; the run continues with the next test case.

This is the behaviour of the fatal assertions of most C++ test frameworks
(`ASSERT_*` in GoogleTest, `REQUIRE()` in Catch2 and doctest,
`BOOST_REQUIRE()` in Boost.Test). The test case is stopped with an
exception; without exceptions, it cannot be stopped alone, so the
results of the test case are reported, ending with the failed
requirement (followed, with the TAP reporter, by a `Bail out!` line),
and the whole test execution is aborted with `runner::abort()`, as
Catch2 does; the remaining test cases do not run, and no totals are
written.
